### Summary (counts and priorities from the output above)
reader at priority 40, interrupt thread while the read waited (pidin): 21 with -f, 40 by default
with 8 loops at priority 30, run 5: 24 of 27 9P requests were completed by the waiting server thread's fallback wait instead of the interrupt thread with -f (highest interrupt-thread priority 21); 0 of 27 by default (highest 40)
fallback completions per run (completed by the fallback wait / 9P requests), 5 runs: with -f: 24/27 24/27 24/27 24/27 24/27 (range 24 to 24); by default: 0/27 0/27 0/27 0/27 0/27 (range 0 to 0)
part 3a (fs-9p -n 1, default, no load): reads at 40 during which a server thread waited on the slot's mutex: 3 of 3; priority of the waiting thread / of the slot's owner (the reader-at-10's server thread) in the first such pidin: 40r/40r
part 3b (fs-9p -n 1, reader at 10 holds the slot, 8 loops at 30, a 256 KiB read at 40), 5 run(s) per mode; loops that had ended when the read at 40 finished (of 8), per run: with -f: 8 8 8 8 8 (range 8 to 8); with -w: 8 8 8 8 8 (range 8 to 8); by default: 0 0 0 0 0 (range 0 to 0)
part 3b, waits for a free slot by a client above every slot owner, per run: -f: 3 3 3 11 5 ; -w: 3 3 10 11 10 ; default: 1 1 1 1 1 | slots handed over: -f: 0 0 0 0 0 ; -w: 0 0 0 0 0 ; default: 1 1 1 1 1 | fallback completions: -f: 0 0 1 0 0 ; -w: 1 1 1 2 1 ; default: 0 1 0 0 0 | md5 of the read at 40 against the host: -f: equal equal equal equal equal ; -w: equal equal equal equal equal ; default: equal equal equal equal equal 
fallback wait: a server thread that waits for the device looks at the ring itself every 200 ms, in case the interrupt thread did not run (a constant in src/virtq.c, not a measurement); the counts say how often that check, not the interrupt thread, completed a request

